    <releases>
      <release type="stable" version="1.8.3" timestamp="1790726400">
        <description>
          <p>The Creeds, and Scripture in Stone in its sources&apos; words.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The Creeds: the Apostles&apos;, Nicene, Chalcedonian and Athanasian Creeds, each line drawn to the verses it comes from.</li>
            <li>Pick a line to follow its threads into the Bible, or a book to see which lines rest on it.</li>
            <li>Each creed sits beside its Greek or Latin original, with old English words explained.</li>
            <li>Disputed lines, and words the Bible does not use, are explained in the words of the Fathers and the confessions.</li>
            <li>A verse a creed quotes carries a small mark that opens the creed at that line.</li>
            <li>Scripture in Stone quotes each stone, or an old witness to it, checked word for word.</li>
            <li>Every verse an artifact bears on shows its text and how the two are linked.</li>
            <li>Contested readings are marked, and the titles are set in the serif.</li>
          </ul>
        </description>
      </release>
      <release type="stable" version="1.8.2" timestamp="1790726400">
        <description>
          <p>Spanish and Russian beyond the menus, and the Bible Family Tree in the Module Manager.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>In Spanish or Russian, a Strong&apos;s entry also shows how a Bible in that language renders the word.</li>
            <li>The places named in a verse take the names a Spanish or Russian Bible gives them.</li>
            <li>Three maps come in Spanish and two in Russian, with an update to the imagery pack.</li>
            <li>The Module Manager shows where each English Bible sits from word for word to free, and opens its card.</li>
            <li>Search finds the deuterocanon: Tobit, Sirach, the Maccabees and the rest.</li>
            <li>A reading plan that ran out can begin again from today, keeping the days you read.</li>
            <li>Bibles that number the Psalms like the Vulgate show the psalm the header names.</li>
            <li>A tip, shown once, says where the footnote switches are.</li>
            <li>Scripture in Stone and the genealogy pages have their wider line spacing back.</li>
            <li>Downloads name Scriptura, where some claimed to be a web browser.</li>
          </ul>
        </description>
      </release>
      <release type="stable" version="1.8.1" timestamp="1790640000">
        <description>
          <p>A writing page that reads like a page, and a view to preach from.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Writing looks like a page: Markdown symbols fold away once you leave a line.</li>
            <li>Paragraph styles and a choice of font for your journal and sermons.</li>
            <li>Tables in the journal and sermons, with Tab to move between cells.</li>
            <li>Preach from a sermon: F5 shows it full screen in large type, with a timer.</li>
            <li>Pasting from a web page or word processor keeps headings, lists, bold and italic.</li>
            <li>Click a verse&apos;s note number to read the note on the page.</li>
            <li>Read the difference follows the Bible beside it.</li>
            <li>Reduce motion in GNOME Settings is now honoured.</li>
            <li>The Today page and startup open without jumps and keep your place.</li>
            <li>Esc behaves in the Annotations window, and word study counts from interlinears.</li>
          </ul>
        </description>
      </release>
      <release type="stable" version="1.8.0" timestamp="1790380800">
        <description>
          <p>The Bible Family Tree, and downloads that look after themselves.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The Bible Family Tree draws the English Bibles people read, and the Bibles they came from, down the page in time. Print it as a poster.</li>
            <li>The Line places every English Bible from word for word to free. Filter it by what you have, by tradition and by era.</li>
            <li>Read the difference shows one verse in every Bible you have, with the Greek or Hebrew above it.</li>
            <li>Each English Bible has a card: where it sits on the Line, the Bibles it came from, and the source of every fact.</li>
            <li>Compare lists its Bibles from word for word to free, and finds the right verse in Bibles that number the Psalms differently.</li>
            <li>Downloads wait their turn, show their progress, can be cancelled, and pick up where they stopped.</li>
            <li>The Module Manager checks for updates when its lists are a week old, and a dot on the menu says when one is waiting.</li>
            <li>Every download is checked for damage before it is installed.</li>
            <li>The menu now holds only places, and a Preferences window holds the settings you set once.</li>
            <li>Scriptura has a website, in English, Spanish and Russian.</li>
          </ul>
        </description>
      </release>
    </releases>
